> I recently bought RAM for my DEC Alpha 200, problem is, everytime I put in
> Ram all I get is beep...beep beep beep...beep beep beep, the manual says it
> can't find any good memory when this happens... problem is, this is my 5th
> set of Ram I bought that does this. I paid $175 for 2-32 meg chips that was
> built specifically for the Alphastation 200, 72-pin, parity, 60 ns. Any ideas
> of what's going on? With all the other Ram I bought it would still work with
> the older ram in the machine. Now it does nothing except beep, even when I've
> got the older Ram in. I'm clueless and frustrated on this, I would really
> like to use more than 32 megs of Ram in this machine, but it doesn't seem to
> like any extra ram.

Sounds like you didn't got real parity memory or you have mixed speed memory.
- Did you try the new memory on it's own (without any other memory simms
installed) ? - Are you sure it's real parity memory and the "parity memory
chip" is not some parity emulation chip ? (These fake-emulation chips have
usually quite similar numbers like real memory). And if you are sure - did
you check this yourself or is this what the dealer told you ? (If you are not
sure, mail me the exact text of all the chips and I might be able to verify)
- Are you installing the simms in pairs with both simms of *EXACTLY* the same
type ? - Are all chips 60ns or did you got a cheap mixed-speed simm (maybe by
accident) ? - Do the simms have the size jumpers installed ? (there is
usually a small solder-jumper field on one side - some jumpers need to be
closed. I've seen some cheap (perfectly fine) simms which didn't had this
jumper set and the alphastaion (and other computers) will not recognize them.


I never had problems with any simms, but I usually don't trust dealers...

- Martin



