Alpha was designed by DEC and copies VMS, which also has "infinity".
What on earth is wrong with a "divide by zero" returning "infinity" as
the answer? After all it is mathematically correct.

By all means make it an option whether to return infinity or an error,
but have you ever tried maths programming with a system that only
returns an error?

Code like "x = y / tan( z)" is a damn nightmare if it is legal and
probable that z might be pi/2. I wrote code to calculate distances on a
grid, and I reckon about half the code was devoted to trapping instances
where the two points I was checking shared the same lat or long, as this
would have triggered a divide by zero. It would have been so much easier
if my compiler supported infinity, because then a following calculation
would have divided by it and returned the answer I wanted (ie d(lat)=0)
without having to put code in to trap that as a special case.

In article <01be0482$6ebd1e60$45934b0c@default>, Shana Tackett
<sltackett@worldnet.att.net> writes
>The Alpha has much better exception handling than the PII. The PII is
>setting the register to INF upon division 
>by zero and continues processing. To me the Alpha is correct. You have
>asked the processor to do something
>which does not make sense and it has reported an error. Whereas the PII has
>returned an answer which has to
>interpreted as being an invalid answer. A lot of sloppy mathematical code
>will run fine on an Intel chip producing
>a result which really has no meaning. This is not so on an Alpha. It is
>better to get no answer than an answer
>which is spurious and can be wrongly interpreted as correct. Hence write
>and develop your code on the alpha this way it
>will be thoroughly tested. There are instructions in AXP which allow you to
>specifiy a function to be called upon
>a floating point exception and have the function handle the error. So it is
>possible to get the same result, although
>I would not want to.
>
>This is one of the main reasons for complications for poorly written
>mathematics code to not be easily ported from
>Intel to Alpha.
>
