 Runing the simulation program Aero (www.ee.uwa.edu.au/~braunl/aero)
under DIGITAL Unix (4.0) compiled with the DIGITAL compiler on a
as200 4/233 ran 1.29 times faster than the same code running under
RedHat5.1 on a 233MHz i586 machine (Micron XKE).

I wanted to have a common development environment, so I decided
to ditch the DIGITAL Unix. (Mostly I was frustrated my license wouldnt
allow me to use a debugger.)
After installing RedHat5.1 for Alpha and compiling the same code
with gcc on the alpha, the alpha code ran 0.45 times as fast as
the i586 machine, even with most optimizations.
 So RedHat5.1 with gcc is 35% as fast as DIGITAL Unix and the
DIGITAL compiler on the same hardware!

Adding -lffm raised the speed to 0.81 times as fast as the i586 machine,

still 63% of the speed of the DIGITAL compiler, and still unexpectedly
slower
than a comperable intel machine.
 -pavlo





