Martin Ostermann (Martin.Ostermann+432|address^*is*^correct+@comnets.RWTH-Aachen.de) wrote:
: In article <1103_909480214@pannuaptiva>,
: 	hgm@saunalahti.fi writes:
: > I'm having problem with XCDRoast 0.96d
: > 
: > When I start writing data to cd (burning) ,XCDRoast says "some 
: > error occured...."  error is "cdrecord-1.6: invalid argument: 
: > shmget failed"
: > 
: > What should I do ?

: Cdrecord >= 1.6 uses by default an shared memory 4 MB fifo, but
: the Alpha kernel has the limit for one piece of shared memory 
: somewhat below 4 MB. You could do the following:

: Patch the kernel and recompile. You probably find the define somewhere 
: in 'linux/asm-alpha/*.h'. Or, patch cdrecord, recompile.

Even simpler solution is "you read manual page, in this case for
cdrecord, and you follow instructions there".  Then you will find that
there exists an environment variable which allows to set a value for
this shared memory segment.  Three line shell script, called 'xcdroast',
which sets this variable and execs "real xcdroast" works just dandy.
A value of 4000k, instead of somewhat bigger 4M, is fine.

  Michal



