Eric Christy wrote:
> 
> hubbardj@earthlink.net wrote:
> 
> > I like www.dynastexpress.com for memory.  I just got two lifetime
> > warranty 32MB ECC SIMMS from them, they turn out to be TI RAM.  Seems
> > like a good price too.
> >
> 
> The as200 will only handle true-parity simms.  ECC should not work in this
> machine.
> 

ECC simms and true parity simms should be identical. It is EDO and
other "logic parity" simms that won't work. All of the Alphas use
the parity bits to run in ECC mode. For 64 bit wide access, ECC
uses 8 bits of Hamming code. Thus 64+8 = 2 x 36bit simms. If the
memory access was 32 bits then each simm would require 38 bits.
The ratio gets progressively worse. By the time you get down to
a 4bit access, you need 3bits of ECC. It has been 20 years since
I had to teach this stuff! Back in the old days we designed the
actual circuits to do Hamming Code Error Checking & Correction.
It is pretty neat. While parity can only detect single bit errors
and does that by failing, double bit errors are passed on to
crash systems and corrupt data. ECC on the otherhand actually 
corrects singlebit errors and detects double bit errors.

Anyway.. the result is that he can use his ECC simms. 

Charlie

