: In the near future I would like to get a new machine, and would like it
: to be alpha.  What I am concerned about is that all my stuff wont run on
: alpha.  I use RH5.1 (I know they make an alpha distro) but I'm talking
: about the 'other stuff' Netscape, Staroffice, etc.  Also, is there a
: Linux x86 emulator for alpha for stuff that hasnt been ported?  
Linux-Alpha has a x86 emulation called em86. This enables Alphas running
Linux to run Linux Intel binaries, but NOT Intel-Windoze binaries and not
dosemu or thelike. Besides this em86 is _very_ slow and should only be used
if you cannot avoid it.
Running bigger apps like netscape or especially StarOffice is not a choice.
Rumors are that a version of Applixware will be released soon for Linux
Alpha. This would be the better way to go (IMHO).

: Once I sort out what software works/compiles and what doesnt, Its 500Mhz
: here I come!
Besides this you should be able to compile and run most packages that come
with sourcecode. Many packages are a bit broken because they use integer
pointer arithmetic, i.e. the old 32 <-> 64 bit problem, but most of them can
be fixed quickly. You should browse the net for existing Linux Alpha binary
RPMS to get an idea what is already running on Alpha.

