Hi Linux friends,

I just compiled POVray 3.01 on my Alpha system (21164 533 Mhz), LX
board, 64MB Ram, Redhat Linux 5.1) and compared the results with the
Windows NT version (binary is available for download at the "Alpha NT
Source").

I added following compiler switches to the CFLAGS defined in the
makefile:
DALIGN=double

Then I finally could compile it with adding:
-O -mieee

with higher optimisation settings -O2 and -O3 i got an compiler error
message (signal 6). Without the -mieee switch i could compile it but it
immediately generated a "floating point exception" and dumped core.

with the command :
x-povray -ichess.pov +D +B1000 res640

it took POVray 44 seconds to render the picture.

I changed the settings to:

-O3 -ffloat-store

and it took POVray just 32 seconds to render the picture.
Another test run with the skyvase.pov file unfortunately created a
"floting point exception" so that solution seemed to be not too stable
even though the speedup was pretty hefty.

BUT,

the Windows NT version just needs 20 seconds !!!!! to render the same
file with the same resolution and settings which makes the egcs compiler
output look like a snake.

Is this just due to the -mieee compiler setting or is the egcs compiler
not mature (to say the least) enough on the alpha platform under Linux
?????

It would be interresting if somebody has expierience and comparable
benchmark results with the DIGITAL compiler under DIGITAL Unix

P. Doering



