I did some experiment with the first two executable lines
which is basically "x = dlog(0.d0) ".

      Program Never
      implicit real*8 (a-h,o-z)

      call FPUSETDEF
      a = 0.d0
      x = dlog(a)
      stop
      end

Then, the following C routine works as desired with Intel/Linux:

/*
 * Set the floating point control to fault on divide by zero etc...
 * instead of returning a NaN. This is *not* IEEE compliant but is much
 * easier to use when debugging.
*/

#include <fpu_control.h>


void
fpusetdef_(void)
{
  __setfpucw(0x1372);
}

However, the above C-routine does not work with Alpha/Linux.
Can someone correctly point out the right floating point
interrupt address (instead of 0x1372 for Intel)?
In /usr/include/fpu_control.h, there is a bunch of important
messages which I don't understand.  If Steve Lionel cannot
help us, then the problem must be some hardware limitation
(or design choice) of Alpha.

BTW, I think I can understand Don Baker's frustration.
Above C-routine for Intel/Linux was provided by David Klein.
When I was working with DECstation 3000 running MIPS FORTRAN,
a similar C-routine was provided by one of  DEC insiders.
When DEC released DEC FORTRAN for MIPS, the interrupt handler
became unnecessary.

With no such tools provided, Alpha/Linux/NT cannot be considered
as a machine for numerical code developments, IMHO.  So I am
using K6-2/Linux and Alpha/Linux side by side.

