Hi all,

first the problem in a few words:

When I run a number-crunching Fortran program using the 2.1.125 kernel, 
the system effectively locks up until the program has finished (i.e., 
all resources are consumed by the Fortran program, I cannot do even the
simplest tasks, but after the program has finished everything is fine 
again).

Now the details:

I have a 533 Mhz alpha, Samsung UX-4 motherboard (aka Ruffian).  
It runs fine (without the above problem) with Redhat 5.1, 2.0.35
kernel.  
Now I want to run DEC Unix v4.0 binaries for which one apparently needs 
a 2.1.xx kernel later than 2.1.23.  I got 2.1.125 and compiled it.  
Now I can run DEC Unix binaries.  However, the above-mentioned problem 
occurs (effective lock-up when running the Fortran program), both for
the DEC Unix binary and the Linux binary.  So it seems that the problem 
has nothing to do with the DEC Unix binary but is caused by the kernel.  
I used both gcc-2.8.1 and egcs-1.1b to compile the 2.1.125 kernel, but
the problem is always there.

Does anyone have an idea what's going on?  I can supply more detailed
information if needed.  Any help would be greatly appreciated.

Thanks,

Tilo Wettig
Institute for Theoretical Physics
Technical University Munich
(Tilo.Wettig@physik.tu-muenchen.de)



