Unless I'm mistaking, the Beowulf systems are linked together with an array
of Ethernet cards (ie. more than one card per computer) so the single
built-in Ethernet of the Multia would not necessarily suffice.

Next, the fact that those old Multia's come without a hard disk or memory
would increase your costs substantially per machine.  Finally, the Multia
is a dog when it comes to speed.  I've heard that the memory subsystem was
made as cheeply as possible.  I use to have an old 166Mhz and it ran NT and
Linux about the speed of a 100Mhz Pentium if even that good.

As far as I'm concerned, if you are developing software (or just want to
say you too were one of the first people on the planet to run a REAL 64-bit
OS) and don't care whatsoever about performance, then a Multia is the
cheapest way to break into architecture.

Murray Todd Williams


