fliu@uci.edu (Feng Liu) writes:

> Toon Moene (mailto:toon@moene.indiv.nluug.nl) wrote:
> 
> > We're unlikely to "repair" this, as that would mean that files produced  
> by 
> > g77-x.y.z would not be readable by g77-x.y.z+1, an even less preferable 
> > situation.
> 
> I would disagree with this! In our place, we have DEC machines with DEC's  
> f77 and f90 under
> DEC unix, Intel machines with g77 under Linux, and Alpha PCs with g77
> under Linux. We share files through NFS. I must say that all my students  
> and myself find it a nuisance to have to keep 2 copies of our data files
> in order to communicate. It is far easier and desirableto convert some old  
> files once and for all rather than perpetuate the problem!

I think Toon meant, by "preferable", preferable for the general user
base of g77 on Alphas, not preferable for every single g77/Alpha user.

Also, I've already posted another, IMO rather important, reason to not go
back to 4-byte headers on Alphas, having to do with not crippling
writing large arrays using unformatted I/O just to default to
compatibility with ancient machines, like 32-bit Pentiums.  :)

g77 really needs to offer (someday) better ways to select the formats
of data files than it currently does.

In the meantime, sites where inter-machine compatibility is important
(and achievable without bit-twiddling), it isn't really all that hard
to patch g77 specifically for those sites to achieve this effect.
E.g. I think there's already a macro in gcc/f/runtime/libI77 (UIOLEN_int
or some such thing), and in egcs/libf2c/libI77, that, when defined,
forces 4-byte headers (or equivalent).
