Richard Maine wrote in message ...
...
>P.S. I *HAVE* seen a non-unix system (Elxsi) that used only headers with no
>trailer or other way to record the size of the preceding record.  Backspace
>even worked - wanna know how?  It kept track of the current record number
>and, if you wanted to backspace, it rewound the file and skipped N-2 records.

Ouch.  Well, in order to get to record N, it should have traversed the
file sequentially.  Why not keep track of the previous record's length
instead of just the record number?  Then a single backspace would
be easy.  Multiple backspaces would still require going back to the
start.  Actually, you could make a trade-off between memory overhead
in the I/O implementation and the time required for backspacing by
keeping the last N record lengths on a stack (throwing away the oldest
one when you try to push more than N) and figuring what value of
N is sufficient for the "average" number of consecutive backspaces.
It cold be made to work I suppose.  Not worth it though: better
to have the backward link.

--
J. Giles





