Well, if you are a CS grad student, I'm going to make the assumption
that you are comfortable with Unix, and by your posting here that
you've probably used Linux at least a little bit, maybe a lot.  The
PII is more versatile than the Alpha under Windows NT, no doubt about
it, but Linux (or any UNIX for that matter) is kind of an equalizer on
the software side.  Pretty much anything that will run on Intel Linux
will also compile and run on Alpha, though a variable amount of
tweaking may be involved, but that probably wouldn't bother you that
much.
  On the hardware side, consider this:  The motherboard and the
processor are all that you will really lose should you decide to
switch platforms in the future.  New Alpha workstations almost
universally use SDRAM, PCI cards, and IDE and/or SCSI hard drives, all
of which will run just as happily on an Intel machine as they will on
an Alpha.  If the price difference between that two is negligable, I'd
definitely go Alpha, but don't get a 21164PC alpha, it'd still be
faster than the Intel, but not as much, get a regular 21164.

