> 4/233 no ARC. So i download the aboot and it will not compile on my
> mac!(linuxppc) so i downloaded a compiled copy from sunsite
> ftp://sunsite.unc.edu/pub/Linux/distributions/redhat/redhat-5.1/alpha/misc/
> bootlx

No, you will not be able to compile aboot, nor should you need to.  I
assume
you have a RH5.1 CD?  It has a compiled bootlx on it (in the /misc dir I
think).
If you have RH5.0 get 5.1.  Trust me, several booting problems related
to SRM
were fixed.

You should not have to compile anything or do anything fancy to make any
boot disks.  There are disk images on the CD.

I would guess that 99% of your frustration is based on SRM.  If you use
the
info provided from RedHat you just will not have success.  Your best bet
is
to install ARC on the machine.

Get the firmware image from: 

	ftp.digital.com:/pub/DEC/Alpha/firmware/v5.2/alpha200/as200_v6_8.exe

also get:

	ftp.digital.com:/pub/DEC/Alpha/firmware/utilities/mkboot.exe
	ftp.digital.com:/pub/DEC/Alpha/firmware/utilities/mkboot.txt

OK, you will need *shudder* a DOS machine for the next step:

format a floppy and run

	mkboot.exe as200_v6_8.exe a:

at least I think that is the correct syntax.

now insert the floppy in your alpha and at the ">>>" prompt type:

>>> b dva0

it will reboot into the firmware update utility.  Select "Update"
then select update "ARC" from the next menu.

NOTE: this is all from memory so the specifics may be off but it
should definitly get you going.

I personally do not use ARC since I need to also be able to boot
Digital UNIX.  But, in many cases it is preferable because that is
what RedHat assumes you are using.


- Doug



