> Yes, we really do.  You can get 32-bit integers on CRAY MPP (T3E) systems,
> but not on the CRAY PVP (C90/T90) systems.
>
> |> I define variables as integer*4.  This should make them 32-bit.
>
> No.  We generally regard integer*n as a request rather than a commandment.
> On CRAY PVP systems it may allow us to use a reduced precision model, but
> the container size will still be 64-bits.
>

It seems that the exact same thing happens with Alpha/Linux g77 fortran.
I was trying to enforce compatibility between Intel/Linux and Alpha/Linux
g77.

Here is what I did:
I created a simple fortran program:
      program OUTtest

      implicit none
      integer   UOUt, i, id(5)
      parameter(Uout = 11)
      integer*2 i2(5)
      integer*4 i4(5)
      real      rd(5)
      real*8    r8(5)

      do i = 1, 5
         i2(i) = i
         id(i) = i
         i4(i) = i
         r8(i) = i
         rd(i) =i
      end do

      open(Uout, file = 'i2.dt', form='unformatted')
      write(Uout) i2
      close(Uout)
      open(Uout, file = 'i4.dt', form='unformatted')
      write(Uout) i4
      close(Uout)
      open(Uout, file = 'id.dt', form='unformatted')
      write(Uout) id
      close(Uout)
       open(Uout, file = 'rd.dt', form='unformatted')
      write(Uout) rd
      close(Uout)
      open(Uout, file = 'r8.dt', form='unformatted')
      write(Uout) r8
      close(Uout)

      stop
      end

Then, I created two executables, test.ix for intel and test.ax for alpha.
The file sizes of the output files after running test.ix are
-rw-------   1 ghsong   faculty        28 Oct 14 11:47 i4.dt
-rw-------   1 ghsong   faculty        28 Oct 14 11:47 id.dt
-rw-------   1 ghsong   faculty        48 Oct 14 11:47 r8.dt
-rw-------   1 ghsong   faculty        28 Oct 14 12:21 rd.dt
-rw-------   1 ghsong   faculty        18 Oct 14 11:47 i2.dt

After running test.ax, they are
-rw-------   1 ghsong   faculty        36 Oct 14 11:35 i4.dt
-rw-------   1 ghsong   faculty        36 Oct 14 11:35 id.dt
-rw-------   1 ghsong   faculty        56 Oct 14 11:35 r8.dt
-rw-------   1 ghsong   faculty        36 Oct 14 12:18 rd.dt
-rw-------   1 ghsong   faculty        26 Oct 14 11:35 i2.dt

This result implies that the two output "record" files are
significantly different.
Even if they both use IEEE-format, there is extra bits of
information appended on the fortran output files must also
be different.

Reading the files from test.ax into IDL running on Intel/Linux
fails with the message such as "Corrupted input data".

It seems that the only solution to this incompatibility problem
is to let C-code handle all the  binary outputs or to use
ascii outputs only, which is unacceptable with huge data files.

