> Nicolas

I use lynx as my primary browser on my Linux Alpha system.  It is much
faster and much more stable than the graphical browsers that are
available.  The version supplied with RedHat 5.1 supports frames and
image maps.

For graphical browsers, Mozilla and KFM (from KDE) both work with
varying degrees of success.  The dialog boxes on Mozilla are horribly
broken, and it isn't very stable, but it is usable for casual browsing. 
I can't remember where I got the binary from.  Sorry.

KFM works okay.  I don't like the way it manages the forward and back
arrows, and it tends to render pages incorrectly, but like Mozilla, it
is usable for casual browsing.

I've heard of a browser for Gnome similar to KFM, but I don't have any
experience with it.

One thing all these lack is SSL support.  I think that Cryptzilla has
this support, but again, I have no experience with it.

Hope this helps.

Richard Griswold - griswold@rconnect.com



