"H.W. Stockman" <hwstock@wizard.com> writes:

> I was under the impression that the exact form of the record
> delimiter (for unformatted files) is not specified by F77.  In
> most FORTRANs I have used, the delimiter is simply an integer
> that corresponds to the record length, as implied in the
> previous discussion.  However, I think other FORTRANs (e.g.
> Lahey) have more complicated record delimiters.  Thus two FORTRANs
> on the same platform may not seemlessly exchange unformatted files--
> que no?

Right. On Unix platforms, which is what this fellow is using, there is
a simple convention: a 4-byte delimiter giving the length of the
record in bytes. I've yet to meet an alternate Fortran compiler on a
Unix box which did anything different; the only incompatibility I've
seen springs from the desire to support 64 bits, and between
platforms, Cray's use of a word length instead of a byte length.

-- g




