In article <36255A0B.4C40@wizard.com>, "H.W. Stockman" <hwstock@wizard.com> writes:
|> Greg Lindahl wrote:
|> [...]
|> > Linux/Alpha writes an 8-byte record length in bytes.
|> >
|> > Linux/x86 writes a 4-byte record length in bytes.
|> >
|> > It is fairly trivial to write a conversion program. There is a
|> > record length at the start and end of each record.
|>
|> I was under the impression that the exact form of the record
|> delimiter (for unformatted files) is not specified by F77.  In
|> most FORTRANs I have used, the delimiter is simply an integer
|> that corresponds to the record length, as implied in the
|> previous discussion.  However, I think other FORTRANs (e.g.
|> Lahey) have more complicated record delimiters.  Thus two FORTRANs
|> on the same platform may not seemlessly exchange unformatted files--
|> que no?

That is correct.  The Fortran standard specifically leaves the structure
of unformatted files up to the individual implementation.  Since the
standard also requires various record positioning capabilities (i.e.,
BACKSPACE and skipping records), that effectively requires that there be
_some_ structure.

