"G. Hugh SONG" <ghsong@\"Spamspoiler\"kjist.ac.kr> wrote:

>       open(Uout, file = 'i2.dt', form='unformatted')
>       write(Uout) i2
>       close(Uout)

        ...

>       open(Uout, file = 'r8.dt', form='unformatted')
>       write(Uout) r8
>       close(Uout)

>       stop
>       end

> Then, I created two executables, test.ix for intel and test.ax for alpha.
> The file sizes of the output files after running test.ix are
> -rw-------   1 ghsong   faculty        28 Oct 14 11:47 i4.dt
> -rw-------   1 ghsong   faculty        28 Oct 14 11:47 id.dt
> -rw-------   1 ghsong   faculty        48 Oct 14 11:47 r8.dt
> -rw-------   1 ghsong   faculty        28 Oct 14 12:21 rd.dt
> -rw-------   1 ghsong   faculty        18 Oct 14 11:47 i2.dt

> After running test.ax, they are
> -rw-------   1 ghsong   faculty        36 Oct 14 11:35 i4.dt
> -rw-------   1 ghsong   faculty        36 Oct 14 11:35 id.dt
> -rw-------   1 ghsong   faculty        56 Oct 14 11:35 r8.dt
> -rw-------   1 ghsong   faculty        36 Oct 14 12:18 rd.dt
> -rw-------   1 ghsong   faculty        26 Oct 14 11:35 i2.dt

> This result implies that the two output "record" files are
> significantly different.

Indeed they are - the Alpha unformatted records have two 64-bit record length 
specifiers, whereas the Intel ones use 32-bit specifiers.

Note that the Fortran Standard doesn't guarantee you anything about the 
possibility of reading unformatted files on one system that were written on 
another; therefore the records in them and their sizes do not necessarily 
have to agree.

For this sort of exchange, use formatted I/O or a portable "binary" format 
like HDF.

Hope this helps,

