"James Giles" <jamesgiles@worldnet.att.net> writes:

> All Fortran I/O libraries I've seen (and I've even written one) have
> a record mark which contains both the length of the next record
> and the length of the preceeding record.

Conceptual difference: I call that 2 record marks. At the start of the
file there is only 1 record mark.

>  On Crays, this was represented
> as a 64-bit quantity: the first 32 were the length of the preceeding
> record and the second 32 were the length of the following record.

I just looked on a Cray and it seems to have changed formats between
f77 and f90. The new format is considerably different.

-- g

unicos% od o
0000000000000 000000 000000 000000 000001 000000 000000 000000 000020
              [       r1 length ?       ] [ record 1 data ]
0000000000020 100000 000000 000000 000001 000000 000000 000000 000021
              [ combo of r1 and r2 len? ] [ record 2 data ]
0000000000040 100000 000000 000000 000000 160000 000000 000000 000000
              [ r2 length?              ]
0000000000060 170000 000000 000000 000000 000000 000000 000000 000000
0000000000100 000000 000000 000000 000000 000000 000000 000000 000000
*
0000000010000

A bit satantic for my taste, with the reverse lengths reversed?

unicos% cat test.f
      program test
      open (unit=21, file = 'o', form='unformatted', status='unknown')
      write (21) 16
      write (21) 17
      close (unit=21)
      stop
      end

32-bit linux% od o
0000000 000004 000000 000020 000000 000004 000000 000004 000000
        ^^^^^^^^^^^^^ ^^^^^^^^^^^^^ ^^^^^^^^^^^^^ ^^^^^^^^^^^^^
        little-endian     data      repeat of      length of 
        length in bytes             r1 length       record 2
0000020 000021 000000 000004 000000
        ^^^^^^^^^^^^^ ^^^^^^^^^^^^^
        record 2 data   repeat of r2 length

64-bit linux% od o
0000000 000004 000000 000000 000000 000020 000000 000004 000000
0000020 000000 000000 000004 000000 000000 000000 000021 000000
0000040 000004 000000 000000 000000
0000050

Note the 32-bit lengths are now 64-bit lengths.



