In article <3622570E.C6D63F11@ipass.net>, N. McNeill <njm@ipass.net> wrote:
>N. McNeill wrote:
>
>>
>> 1. what does the 4 stand for (4)/233 ?
>>
Alpha chips were/are produced in 'generations' based on the fabrication
method and die size used.  The 4 stands for EV4, which was the first 
commercial runs of the Alpha chip.  The AS200 4/166 used the 21064 chip,
the 4/233 reportedly used the EV45 based (EV4 design produced on upcoming 
EV5-sized (smaller) feature size), but was still considered a 4/xxx.
EV5 chips are the 21164 still in use today (as EV56) in currently
produced Alpha products.

>> 2. where can I get memory; hard drives; modems etc... (there was no
>> mention of a HD when I bought it at onsale.com) ?


The system can support two internal 3.5" _narrow_ single ended hard
disks; I've heard that 5400RPM drives are recommended as the system does
not have sufficient cooling to handle 7200RPM drives.  One can be a half-
height, the other must be a 1/3 height (1" tall).  There is a bay for 
adding a SCSI CD-ROM or tape drive (half height 5.25").  Onsale tends to
have the CDRoms, but has not had much for the lower speed hard drives
lately.

Memory is X by 36bit true parity (NOT logic parity), six slots that you
must fill in matched pairs.  ONsale has had 4x36 (16MB) Simms on and off,
and I saw 16x36 (64MB) Simms there today; I have a pair of the 64MB
Simms in my AS200 4/166 right now, and tested them in a Multia; the
Multias are now running on the 16MB Simms that were in the AS200 and
all are happy; this is ALL ONsale memory, though from several months ago.

Rich Jordan
rjordan@mcs.net



