From: Matt Grossman <mattg@oz.net>
Subject: UDB and 3c905b ethernet card
Date: 1998/10/11
Message-ID: <m3hfxafp9r.fsf@mattg.oz.net>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 400106764
Sender: mattg@mattg.oz.net
Organization: Sense Networking Seattle (http://www.oz.net)
Newsgroups: comp.os.linux.alpha


I recently obtained a 3c905b (3Com Fast Etherlink XL) and installed it
into a UDB.  Yes, Redhat says it's not supported, but it seems to
_almost_ work with kernel 2.0.35, so I thought I would post my
experiences and see if anyone else had tried something similar and has
any tips, or that someone else might find this information useful.

1) I tested the driver by ftping an ~50MB file (uncompressed linux
kernel source) from the UDB.  Unfortunately, it would hang, sometimes
for several minutes every 30MB or so.  I altered the definition of
TX_TIMEOUT in /usr/src/linux/drivers/net/3c59x.c like so:

#define TX_TIMEOUT ((4 * HZ)/1000)

This seemed to fix that problem, although I now get messages like:

 eth1: transmit timed out, tx_status 00 status e000

the connection picks right back up again.  I suspect I'm fixing the
symptom not the problem.

2) When ftping _to_ the UDB, the hub shows a lot of collisions, and
performance is bad (this is with only two machines on the hub).  But
it does work.

3) I have seen two messages like:
Oct 11 14:52:43 mattg kernel: lca: machine check (la=0xfffffc00002084d0,pc=0xfffffc0000402854)
Oct 11 14:52:43 mattg kernel:   Reason: SIO SERR occurred on on PCI bus (long frame):
Oct 11 14:52:43 mattg kernel:     reason: fffffc01c01001c0  exc_addr: fffffc0000402854  dc_stat: 3

followed by a hex dump of some kind.  It does not seem to cause any
harm :-)

I should mention that the UDB builtin ethernet also works flawlessly
with the 3c905b installed.

I did try the most recent drivers from
http://cesdis.gsfc.nasa.gov/linux/drivers/vortex.html however they did
not work as well as the one that came with 2.0.35.

Matt Grossman
mattg@oz.net
http://www.oz.net/~mattg



