"Ric Stattin" <rstattin@parkcityus.com> writes:
> I have been looking around for info on how to get ecgs up and going on an
> DEC alpha lx running Red Hat 5.1 and have not had much luck.

First: have you installed GCC+includes+libraries that came with Red Hat?
If not, do that now.

Go to http://egcs.cygnus.com. Read some of the introductory material.

Get the current full release (1.1b right now, I think) from 
   ftp://egcs.cyngus.com/pub/egcs/releases/

Do not get a development snapshot. Do not get a componenent release or
the "core" package; get the archive that has everything in one big
wad. For example:
   ftp://egcs.cyngus.com/pub/egcs/releases/egcs-1.1b/egcs-1.1b.tar.gz

Unpack the archive. Read the README and the installation instructions.

Do what they say. You will probably end up doing something like:
  % ./configure --prefix=/some/reasonable/place --enable-haifa --enable-shared
  % make
  # make install

"gcc -v" should now tell you that you are using the version of egcs
you downloaded. If you see your old version instead, make sure you
put the new one in a place which is in your path before the old one.
Also make sure that if your shell has the location of the old one
hashed, you clear that out. In bash you can do that with "hash -r".

Make sure you can compile a simple C program. (You also should have
Fortran and C++, if you care about those things.)

I've been using egcs-1.1b with HAIFA scheduling turned on, and with
the "-O9 -fexpensive-optimizations" flags for a little over a week,
and have experienced no lossage yet (well, none that had to do with
the compiler, anyway).

The aforementioned EGCS web site has information about mailing lists
you can join if you are really interested in egcs arcana and news.

Also, if you are doing lots of floating point, you will almost
certainly want to investigate alternatives to the GNU libm. Do a
dejanews search on this forum for specifics.

-- 
