Hi, sorry for getting back to you only now, I was too busy with all
kinds of stuff, unfortunatelly not only alpha programming..... Actually,
I can call myself pretty new to Alpha assembler programming, too.
However, I already did much "high performance" assembler programming on
other CPUs before. So, I had not yet too much time getting any regular
books on this issue on the Alpha (even if DEC seems to have published
some). HOWEVER, most of the "real" stuff to go with assembler
programming on the Alpha is available free of charge on the web, and
that's what I use. You simply need a nice PS and/or PDF reader/converter
to print all that stuff out and need to get it spiral bound or so
(Several 100s of pages). I recommend going through the hassle to print
it double-sided, use psselect or the like, you have a much more compact
documentation (and you save trees, a bit at least)

OK, here are the links:

Look at

http://www.unix.digital.com/faqs/publications/base_doc/DOCUMENTATION/V40D_PDF/

I especially recommend:

APS31DTE.PDF            Assembly Language Programmer's Guide

and also useful might be:

APY8ACTE.PDF            Digital UNIX Calling Standard for Alpha Systems
and
APUBXCTK.PDF            Digital Portable Mathematics Library


Also very helpful might be, on

http://gatekeeper.dec.com/pub/digital/DECinfo/semiconductor/literature/

alphahb2.pdf            Alpha Architecture Handbook Version 3
and
21164hrm.pdf  21164 Hardware Reference Manual


