>be handled somehow allowing the system to keep on running, they do make
>me uncomfortable though.
>> 
>> Yesterday, I've seen that nearly every application is posting me four
>> 'unaligned trap'-messages in my /var/log/messages-file.  This occurs
>> mainly when I'm runnig X (manager: KDE, build with egcs-1.0.3a and
>> qt-1.33): even when I resize a window, there are four new traps... What
>> makes me wondering is, that no application terminates- but  I don't
>> think that such messages are a sign for a proper system.


Somewhere in the Alpha docs it mentions these traps.  They are what their
name implies -- accesses to objects whose address modulo size is non-zero.
The kernel traps these accesses and "fixes" them, allowing life to continue.

Obviously, you take a substantial performance hit when this happens (I have
no idea how much, but likely on the order of 100x slower access), but if it only
happens once every 1000000 instructions, your only talking a slowdown
of about 0.01% -- hardly noticable in real world applications ;-)

Still, tracking down and eliminating the errors would be very "complete" of
you, since they ARE errors.

I got a lot of them on the lx164 box I had.  I don't ever see them on the AS400
I have now.

    - Matt Goheen

